## Code Review: Transaction Partial Commit Bug

### Root Cause Analysis

**Highest-Risk Bug:** The `confirm()` method in `OrderService` performs three separate database operations across different repositories without a transaction boundary. This creates a **partial commit vulnerability**.

**Failure Scenarios:**
| Step 1 | Step 2 | Step 3 | Result |
|--------|--------|--------|--------|
| ✓ Reserve | ✗ Audit fails | - | Inventory reserved, no audit trail |
| ✓ Reserve | ✓ Audit | ✗ Mark confirmed | Inventory reserved, audit exists, order NOT confirmed |

The system can end up in an inconsistent state where inventory is consumed but the order isn't confirmed, or audit logs are missing.

---

### Smallest Safe Fix

**File: `src/main/java/com/example/order/OrderService.java`**

```java
package com.example.order;

import java.time.Instant;
import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional;

public class OrderService {
    private final InventoryRepository inventoryRepository;
    private final OrderRepository orderRepository;
    private final AuditRepository auditRepository;

    public OrderService(
            InventoryRepository inventoryRepository,
            OrderRepository orderRepository,
            AuditRepository auditRepository) {
        this.inventoryRepository = inventoryRepository;
        this.orderRepository = orderRepository;
        this.auditRepository = auditRepository;
    }

    @Transactional  // <-- ADD THIS
    public void confirm(long orderId, int quantity) {
        inventoryRepository.reserve(orderId, quantity);
        auditRepository.record("ORDER_RESERVED", orderId, Instant.now());
        orderRepository.markConfirmed(orderId, Instant.now());
    }
}
```

---

### Why This Fix Is Enough

1. **Atomicity**: The `@Transactional` annotation ensures all three operations execute within a single database transaction. Either all commit, or all roll back.

2. **Minimal Change**: Only one annotation added. No architectural redesign, no saga pattern, no compensating transactions needed.

3. **Automatic Rollback**: If any repository method throws an exception (or the transaction fails for any reason), the database automatically rolls back all changes made within that transaction.

4. **Consistency Guaranteed**: After the fix, the system will never be in a partial state.

---

### Regression Checks

| Test Case | Expected Behavior (After Fix) |
|-----------|------------------------------|
| `confirm()` with successful operations | All 3 records committed |
| `confirm()` with inventory reservation failure | No records created (rollback) |
| `confirm()` with audit recording failure | No records created (rollback) |
| `confirm()` with order mark failure | No records created
